---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const title = "Focus-Following Explorer | PaneCrew Docs";
const description =
  "How PaneCrew's file explorer automatically tracks whichever terminal pane currently has focus — and why that's the app's core mechanic, not a secondary feature.";
---
<DocsLayout title={title} description={description} path="/docs/focus-following-explorer/" active="focus-following-explorer">
  <article class="docs-md">
    <h1>Focus-following explorer</h1>
    <p>
      PaneCrew shows one file explorer for the whole window, not one per pane. That explorer
      always displays the project tree of whichever pane currently has focus — click a different
      pane, and the explorer switches with it, automatically.
    </p>

    <h2>Why this exists</h2>
    <p>
      Running several real projects side by side only works if you can always tell, at a glance,
      which files belong to which terminal. Multi-root workspaces in typical editors either stack
      every open project's files into one long tree, or keep a single fixed explorer that doesn't
      know which of several open terminals you're currently looking at — you end up scrolling or
      manually re-selecting a folder to line the explorer back up with the pane you're actually
      working in. <!-- brandlint-ok: functional description of a common editor pattern, no product named -->
      PaneCrew removes that step entirely: the explorer's context is derived from pane focus, not
      from a separate selection you have to maintain yourself.
    </p>

    <h2>What "focus" means here</h2>
    <p>
      A pane gets focus the same way any window region does — click into its terminal, or move
      focus to it via a <a href="/docs/shortcuts/">keyboard shortcut</a>. The moment a pane has
      focus, the explorer re-targets to that pane's project root. Switching panes and switching
      the visible file tree are the same action, not two separate ones.
    </p>

    <h2>Why it's the core mechanic, not a bolt-on</h2>
    <p>
      This behavior is treated as PaneCrew's central differentiator rather than a secondary
      convenience feature. It's also a real, still-open gap in editors that already support
      multiple simultaneously visible terminals or panes — confirmed by a long-standing open issue in the reference editor's own tracker (<a href="https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/issues/71641" target="_blank" rel="noopener">microsoft/vscode#71641</a>) asking for exactly this. <!-- brandlint-ok: factual reference to a real, verified public GitHub issue (microsoft/vscode#71641), used as competitive-gap evidence per docs/decisions.md -->
    </p>

    <h2>What it doesn't do</h2>
    <p>
      The explorer doesn't merge or stack multiple projects' files together, and it doesn't run
      any hidden automation (no automatic git worktrees, no background branching) to make this
      work — panes simply point at folders you already have checked out, and the explorer just
      follows which one is focused. <!-- brandlint-ok: functional product-principle claim, matches PRODUCT.md "no hidden automation" -->
    </p>
  </article>
</DocsLayout>
